STEP 5 -- the interface form of the command, `{<gateway-ip>|<interface>}`         PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config)# no ip route 10.70.70.0/24 10.0.61.2 255
awplus(config)# ip route 10.70.70.0/24 vlan61
awplus# show running-config | include ip route
ip route 10.70.70.0/24 vlan61
awplus# show ip route database
S    *> 10.70.70.0/24 [1/0] is directly connected, vlan61   <-- installed, selected, in FIB

  arpleg.py --tag L5-interface-form (subject plus the instrument control)
  cap L5-interface-form-eth3.pcap: 20 frame(s)
    x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.10.5 -> 10.70.70.1     [ours]
    x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.10.5 -> 192.168.10.1   [ours]
    x5  ICMP t0/c0 192.168.10.1  -> 192.168.10.5  [ours]        <-- instrument alive
    x5  ICMP t3/c1 192.168.10.1  -> 192.168.10.5  [ours]

  10.70.70.1 : 5 sent, 0 echo replies, and five ICMP type 3 CODE 1 (host
               unreachable), one per packet -- against type 3 CODE 0 (network
               unreachable) in every earlier no-route leg.

  OBSERVATION (correct behaviour, worth recording): the interface form makes the
  prefix ON-LINK, so the DUT ARPs for the destination instead of using a next hop.
  `is directly connected, vlan61` in the FIB means the DUT tried to resolve
  10.70.70.1 by ARP on vlan61. swi_b does not answer ARP for its own loopback
  address on that subnet, so resolution failed and the DUT answered HOST
  unreachable rather than NETWORK unreachable -- the opposite failure from "no
  route". The interface form is therefore for point-to-point links; on a
  VLAN/broadcast interface it is not a substitute for the next-hop form. The rate
  limit seen on the network-unreachable legs (2 per 10) did not apply here (5 per
  5), another difference between the two paths.

  => STEP 5 PASS: the interface form of `ip route` is accepted, installed in the
     config, selected and placed in the FIB (`*>`, `is directly connected, vlan61`),
